      "narrative": "Mara Venn sets down her cup, the clay thudding against the scarred oak. She studies you for a long moment \u2014 not suspicious, just measuring. Then she leans forward, elbows on the table, and speaks low enough that the other patrons' chatter covers her words.\n\n\"The Ash Gate has been sealed for thirty years. The Kestrel Guild locked it after the Ferrying stopped \u2014 after Old Bram died on that side, and whatever came back through wasn't him anymore.\" She taps a finger on the wood. \"The lock itself was Toran Vex's work. He's the one who forged the mechanism that holds it shut. Seven wards, he told me once. Three visible, four hidden. If you try to force it without the keys, the gate doesn't just stay locked \u2014 it *reacts.*\"\n\nShe glances toward the bar, where Toran Vex is wiping the same spot on the counter with nervous, repetitive motions, ink-stained fingers working the rag.\n\n\"And it's closed because the Guild wants it closed. Whatever lies beyond that arch, they decided it stays there. But people talk. Couriers go missing on the road near it. Lights flicker in the arch at midnight, they say, when there's no moon.\" She meets your eyes. \"That's what I know. Toran knows the lock itself. If you want to open it, he's the one who can tell you whether that's even possible.\"\n\nShe picks her cup back up, letting the conversation settle.\n\nWhat do you want to do?"
    },
