      "narrative": "The door of the Copper Finch swings shut behind you, cutting off the low murmur of voices and the clink of cups. The morning air hits you \u2014 damp, cool, carrying the smell of wet stone and something older, like turned earth after a long drought.\n\nThe street is a narrow lane of mud and cobbles, lined with leaning buildings whose upper stories almost touch overhead. Few people are about at this hour. A woman hauling a bucket glances at you, then away. A dog noses through a pile of refuse in a doorway.\n\nThe Ash Gate isn't hard to find. You follow the road as it curves past the last few houses, then opens onto a clearing where the ground turns from cobble to cracked earth. And there it stands: a black arch of stone, perhaps fifteen feet tall and ten wide, set into nothing \u2014 no wall, no structure, just the arch against the grey sky. The stone is dark with damp, streaked with pale mineral deposits like old scars. Beyond the arch, through the empty frame, you can see more grey sky and the suggestion of a hillside, but the view wavers strangely, like heat haze on a cold morning.\n\nThe ground around the gate is bare earth, scuffed and trodden. No grass grows within ten feet of the arch. A single figure sits on a flat stone beside the gate, wrapped in a travel-stained cloak, a courier's satchel resting at their feet. They look up as you approach.\n\nThe figure is **Sable Quill** \u2014 a courier with sharp features and the watchful stillness of someone who's spent a long time waiting.\n\nThey don't rise. They just watch you approach, eyes tracking your movement.\n\nThe gate looms behind them, silent and waiting."
    },
